<span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Buying vs Renting</span>
<h1>Expats: Rent vs Buy Decision for Hanoi Motorbikes | Hanoi Motorbike Guide</h1>
<p>Every expat in Hanoi meets the same question within a season of arriving, usually at the moment a third monthly rental payment leaves the account: should I just buy a machine instead? The question feels like arithmetic, and parts of it are, but the honest answer hangs on two things a spreadsheet cannot price, the length of the stay and the temperament of the rider, and this guide refuses to pretend otherwise. What it can do is lay out both sides without inventing ownership numbers, quote only the rental figures that are actually verified, and hand the live questions to a desk that rents machines and never sells them, which makes its briefing the only neutral one in the city.</p>
<p>The machines, the briefing and the honest numbers come from Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu in Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily 09:00 to 21:00, where the desk briefs expats on both sides of the question honestly, because the shop rents machines and never sells them. Begin at the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-for-sale/">motorbike for sale hub</a>, study the long-stay arrangements on the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly motorbike rental</a> page, check the verified figures on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> hub, review the <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> for maintenance and safety, and keep <a href="/renting-a-motorbike-in-vietnam/">renting a motorbike in Vietnam</a> open for the legal layer.</p>
<nav class="toc">
<ol>
